{"name":"Ocelot","hex":"#f1e2c9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocelot","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An American feline carnivore (Leopardus pardalis, syn. Felis pardalis) covered with blackish ocellated spots and blotches which are variously arranged."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocelot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocelot","blocks":[{"text":"The ocelot is a medium-sized spotted wild cat that reaches 40–50 cm (16–20 in) at the shoulders and weighs between 7 and 15.5 kg on average. It is native to the southwestern United States, Mexico, Central and South America, and the Caribbean islands of Trinidad and Margarita. Carl Linnaeus scientifically described it in 1758. Two subspecies are recognized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocelot","wikidata":"Q33261"},{"title":"Ocelot (vehicle)","blocks":[{"text":"The General Dynamics Ocelot, originally known as the Force Protection Ocelot, is a British infantry mobility vehicle that replaced the United Kingdom's Snatch Land Rover with British forces. It received the service name Foxhound, in line with the canine names given to other wheeled armoured vehicles in current British use such as Mastiff, Wolfhound, and Ridgeback, which are all variants of the Cougar."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Foxhound_Light_Protected_Patrol_Vehicle_%28LPPV%29_MOD_45155791.jpg/330px-Foxhound_Light_Protected_Patrol_Vehicle_%28LPPV%29_MOD_45155791.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocelot_(vehicle)","wikidata":"Q4340588"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo